So my university,
the Richard Ivey School of Business,

00:16:01.411 --> 00:16:06.378
is actually the second largest producer of
case studies in the world behind Harvard.

00:16:06.378 --> 00:16:11.301
So it's exactly the opposite of Brown in
many ways, right, what Rebecca was saying.

00:16:11.301 --> 00:16:12.942
We didn't have that much flexibility.

00:16:12.942 --> 00:16:15.602
All of our class were taught
[INAUDIBLE] cases, and

00:16:15.602 --> 00:16:19.800
we didn't have that choice because
it started really in the third year.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:24.430
And I knew I wanted to go back to
school again and broaden my horizons.

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:31.142
And out of all the programs, it was clear
to me that Stanford was the best for me.

00:16:31.142 --> 00:16:34.091
And I'll get into that a little bit later.

00:16:34.091 --> 00:16:38.319
But the big thing for me at the time
was that it was the right time.

00:16:38.319 --> 00:16:40.684
I had spent two years in
New York out of McKinsey.

00:16:40.684 --> 00:16:47.142
So I had gotten that experience already,
and I was honestly a little bit burnt out.

00:16:47.142 --> 00:16:50.254
I had been leading these
impact investments.

00:16:50.254 --> 00:16:53.843
And I think I went to travel across
30 countries in two years or

00:16:53.843 --> 00:16:55.818
something ridiculous like that.

00:16:55.818 --> 00:17:02.074
I was flying all over, spending a lot of
time in parts of Africa looking at deals.

00:17:02.074 --> 00:17:09.195
And I knew that, okay, this feels like the
right time to go back to school, recharge,

00:17:09.195 --> 00:17:15.072
and learn a lot of different things,
and consider if I do want to pivot.

00:17:15.072 --> 00:17:18.155
And, specifically,
I was in London at the time and

00:17:18.155 --> 00:17:22.616
I wanted to make that transition back
to North America where my family is.

00:17:22.616 --> 00:17:26.650
Tactically my GMATs, I scored well,
but they were about to expire.

00:17:26.650 --> 00:17:29.382
Four years had passed,
and so this was the year.

00:17:29.382 --> 00:17:31.365
And when I looked at schools, Stanford,

00:17:31.365 --> 00:17:33.758
was just clearly the one
that I was interested in.

00:17:33.758 --> 00:17:36.960
I'd work with founders for
a couple of years.

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:43.036
I had that entrepreneurial itch and
still really wanted to build something.

00:17:43.036 --> 00:17:46.040
And at the time because I
was investing in education,

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:50.300
there was a dual education MBA
program that I was considering.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:52.172
I knew that there was
a lot of flexibility or

00:17:52.172 --> 00:17:54.840
opportunities to take
classes across the street.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:59.704
Culture wise was this focus on empathy and
being able to work on the soft

00:17:59.704 --> 00:18:04.330
skills in addition to the hard skills,
it was a bit of a no brainer.

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:07.805
So out of all the schools I applied to,

00:18:07.805 --> 00:18:12.072
by far Stanford was the one
that I was excited to join.

00:18:12.072 --> 00:18:17.515
&gt;&gt; Anyone else have thoughts, please
feel free to chime in at any point too.

00:18:17.515 --> 00:18:23.349
Well, I'll go to the next question here,
and maybe Jackie you can start us off.

00:18:23.349 --> 00:18:29.135
And Rebecca, if you have comments, or
Emily as well, feel free to chime in.

00:18:29.135 --> 00:18:33.302
But were there any academic courses at
Stanford that kind of forced you to

00:18:33.302 --> 00:18:37.484
stretch yourself in new ways or
maybe helped enable your success today?

00:18:37.484 --> 00:18:40.541
Jackie, do you want to get us started?

00:18:40.541 --> 00:18:44.173
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely,
one of the most helpful courses for

00:18:44.173 --> 00:18:46.347
me was a winning writing course.

00:18:46.347 --> 00:18:49.381
Stanford has a lecturer who for 35 years,

00:18:49.381 --> 00:18:54.793
he's been an editor with the New York
Times, and he's helped between 10 and

00:18:54.793 --> 00:18:58.322
12 different Pulitzer Prize
winning articles.

00:18:58.322 --> 00:19:02.849
And we have him as, not just
a teacher of our writing class, but

00:19:02.849 --> 00:19:05.505
as an incredible mentor and friend.

00:19:05.505 --> 00:19:06.942
He really gets to know the students.

00:19:06.942 --> 00:19:12.702
And so I took his class and he sent an
op-ed that I wrote to the New York Times,

00:19:12.702 --> 00:19:17.478
which ended up being published a week or
two after I graduated.

00:19:17.478 --> 00:19:21.415
And that was about discrimination toward
women sexual wellness businesses.

00:19:21.415 --> 00:19:26.575
He also brought Nick Kristoff to our
class and set up a call between me and

00:19:26.575 --> 00:19:30.970
one of the most famous journalists,
which was incredible.

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:34.180
And the faculty really
want to help you succeed.

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:37.330
So he's right now helping as
a recommender on a fellowship.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:38.113
So I loved that course.

00:19:38.113 --> 00:19:42.495
I also really liked, we have a professor
named Laura Arriaga Andreessen who created

00:19:42.495 --> 00:19:46.148
a women in leadership course and
has a longtime philanthropy course.

00:19:46.148 --> 00:19:47.416
But outside of her class,

00:19:47.416 --> 00:19:51.290
she would have dinners at her house with
Arianna Huffington and Darren Walker.

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:54.705
Had a guest at every week
at her house at some point,

00:19:54.705 --> 00:19:57.651
because she was such an incredible mentor.

00:19:57.651 --> 00:20:01.561
The other two quickly I'll mention is I
really liked that there were two classes

00:20:01.561 --> 00:20:03.378
about impact investing that I took.

00:20:03.378 --> 00:20:07.829
And then we also had a class about how to
negotiate term sheets as an angel investor

00:20:07.829 --> 00:20:10.120
or venture capitalist, which I think for

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:14.526
classmates that were going to go work at
a VC firm, might have been less relevant.

00:20:14.526 --> 00:20:18.101
But for those that wanted to do
more entrepreneurial investing,

00:20:18.101 --> 00:20:20.766
the term sheet classes
mattered in particular.

00:20:20.766 --> 00:20:24.339
And the faculty would normally charge
thousands of dollars an hour as

00:20:24.339 --> 00:20:28.218
a consultant, but you got to have his
office hours for free as a student, or

00:20:28.218 --> 00:20:29.400
included in tuition.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:36.720
&gt;&gt; Rebecca, over to you, are there any
academic experiences that stand out?

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:41.368
&gt;&gt; So, I was in the inaugural class for
a class that's still going and

00:20:41.368 --> 00:20:45.360
has grown called designing for
extreme affordability.

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:50.972
And that was basically,
we were partnered with a specific

00:20:50.972 --> 00:20:56.783
community-based organization,
in this case it was in Southern India.

00:20:56.783 --> 00:21:03.484
And focused over the course of a semester
working with engineering students and

00:21:03.484 --> 00:21:08.197
other students across depending
on kind of who came in.

00:21:08.197 --> 00:21:12.904
Law students across
the campus on coming up with

00:21:12.904 --> 00:21:18.081
a business plan for and
prototyping a water system,

00:21:18.081 --> 00:21:22.916
in this case for farmers for
less than $1 a day.

00:21:22.916 --> 00:21:31.266
And I still think about that in
launching the housing accelerator fund.

00:21:31.266 --> 00:21:34.096
And it was before design
thinking was like so

00:21:34.096 --> 00:21:37.397
much the thing and
rapid iterations and fail fast.

00:21:37.397 --> 00:21:43.183
It was very early edge, which I think you
often get, which is so awesome at the GSB.

00:21:43.183 --> 00:21:48.240
The earliest edge thinking and
approach to how do we think and

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:54.800
create things and work together across
different sectors in different ways?

00:21:54.800 --> 00:21:59.902
And so I loved that class and
it will definitely echo what Jackie said

00:21:59.902 --> 00:22:05.289
about the access overall to
the professors, and the faculty dinners.

00:22:05.289 --> 00:22:09.679
I have a beloved professor, Jim Van Horn,
who taught corporate finance for

00:22:09.679 --> 00:22:13.110
decades, which is another
favorite class of mine.

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:17.217
I still think about it every board that
I sit on when I actually understand how

00:22:17.217 --> 00:22:20.040
the income statement and
the cash flow statement and

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:22.046
the balance sheet all fit together.

00:22:22.046 --> 00:22:24.510
I thank Jim Van Horn for that.

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:28.531
And especially my running
my own organization now.

00:22:28.531 --> 00:22:32.864
And we still send each other
cards every holiday season and

00:22:32.864 --> 00:22:35.450
he writes a lot in them.

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:39.624
So I think those kinds of
connections that you can build

00:22:39.624 --> 00:22:43.720
with the professors were
is really a highlight also.

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:47.264
&gt;&gt; Emily, how about you, anything that
stood out from an academic class or

00:22:47.264 --> 00:22:47.971
experience?

00:22:47.971 --> 00:22:51.741
&gt;&gt; All of those, I had some commonalities
with what Jackie and Rebecca had said and

00:22:51.741 --> 00:22:52.704
completely agree.

00:22:52.704 --> 00:22:58.733
The only one that I would add I found
incredibly impactful was Touchy Feely.

00:22:58.733 --> 00:23:04.120
So, at the GSB, we have a course
that is nicknamed Touchy Feely.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.130
It's called interpersonal dynamics.

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:09.992
And, in large part, it's kind of group
therapy with a bunch of MBA students,

00:23:09.992 --> 00:23:11.540
which is kind of scary.

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:16.940
And, it tends to be one of the most
impactful classes that folks have.

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:20.657
I think, for me, Stanford,
it was great to have so

00:23:20.657 --> 00:23:25.332
many classes where there were nuts and
bolts learning, right?

00:23:25.332 --> 00:23:28.805
Learning finance, similar to Rebecca,
I have a liberal arts background.

00:23:28.805 --> 00:23:32.316
So that was really important for
me to get some of that experience,

00:23:32.316 --> 00:23:36.030
understanding logistics,
understanding modeling and marketing.

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:39.871
But ultimately, I think Stanford does
such a good job developing leaders, and

00:23:39.871 --> 00:23:41.890
that's really what that class is about.

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:43.886
It's kind hard to describe, but

00:23:43.886 --> 00:23:47.320
it is one that I think a lot of
people get something out of.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:49.000
It's very unique to Stanford.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:52.350
And that was one that I got a lot out of.

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:53.932
And honestly every day, every week,

00:23:53.932 --> 00:23:56.660
there's something that comes up
that I learned in Touchy Feely.

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:03.708
All right, this next question I'll
pose to Jack then send over to Yuning.

00:24:03.708 --> 00:24:07.842
Were there any Stanford clubs or
extracurricular activities that you

00:24:07.842 --> 00:24:12.533
participated in that maybe helped you
explore or think about new career paths or

00:24:12.533 --> 00:24:14.018
interests that you had.

00:24:14.018 --> 00:24:16.597
Jack maybe starting with you?

00:24:16.597 --> 00:24:21.946
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I came in with kind of a big
company background in consulting in

00:24:21.946 --> 00:24:27.865
private equity firm and even in politics
I worked for a large organization.

00:24:27.865 --> 00:24:33.870
And I was really interested in exploring,
doing something again in politics,

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:38.004
so I spent some time in
the government politics group.

00:24:38.004 --> 00:24:43.800
Like for example, we went and knocked on
doors in Nevada, which was really cool.

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:49.735
I also spent some time in the
entrepreneurship-type clubs in food and

00:24:49.735 --> 00:24:52.359
ag, where I ended up working.

00:24:52.359 --> 00:24:56.171
And I think that sort of
morphed over time into more and

00:24:56.171 --> 00:25:01.604
more just the informal extracurricular
that's very common of working on.

00:25:01.604 --> 00:25:06.390
Whether it's some kind of side project
that's professional in nature, or

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:08.932
starting a company or things like that,

00:25:08.932 --> 00:25:13.060
that many of my classmates were
doing simultaneous with school.

00:25:13.060 --> 00:25:16.403
And I think the club sort of
provided the foundation for

00:25:16.403 --> 00:25:20.700
that where you're meeting other
people that have shared interests.

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:26.280
And then, my experience was over the two
years, it became more and more real and

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:31.304
more and more time-intensive and
kind of led into what I'm doing now.

00:25:31.304 --> 00:25:36.082
So the clubs were super helpful for
me to just explore spaces that I didn't

00:25:36.082 --> 00:25:39.409
know as well or
find people with shared interests.

00:25:39.409 --> 00:25:43.871
And then the other extracurricular
thing I was going to mention is,

00:25:43.871 --> 00:25:47.682
maybe this is outside of
the scope of this question, but

00:25:47.682 --> 00:25:52.708
we did a few GSTs and other travel,
that were just massively important for

00:25:52.708 --> 00:25:57.480
us to feel a closer connection
with some of our classmates.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:01.808
I think we sort of met everyone
quickly and it's 400 people on

00:26:01.808 --> 00:26:06.228
[INAUDIBLE] when you get there and
it can be a little overwhelming.

00:26:06.228 --> 00:26:11.332
But then traveling for a couple of
weeks with a smaller group of 10 or

00:26:11.332 --> 00:26:14.676
20 really forged some
bonds that my wife and

00:26:14.676 --> 00:26:19.530
I still talk to the people that we
went to some of these trips with.

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:23.374
So I don't know that that's quite in
the scope of extracurriculars, but

00:26:23.374 --> 00:26:26.130
it was really important to our experience.

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:30.651
&gt;&gt; Yeah, those global study trips I
think are highlights for many students,

00:26:30.651 --> 00:26:31.880
Yuning over to you.

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:36.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I was involved in our arts,
media, and entertainment club.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:41.749
I ended up organizing and leading
the Future of Media conference that we

00:26:41.749 --> 00:26:46.740
have every year, my first year,
and moderating some panels on it.

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:52.551
So Scooter Braun came during our year,
who's the main agent for Justin Bieber,

00:26:52.551 --> 00:26:57.591
and honestly I learned way more about
how he kind of fell out of favor and

00:26:57.591 --> 00:27:01.534
then came back to the roast
than I ever thought I would.

00:27:01.534 --> 00:27:07.269
But in addition to clubs, I ended up
attending a ton of talks throughout

00:27:07.269 --> 00:27:12.060
campus, so not just at the GSB,
but often in other fields.

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:16.654
So, in particular, whenever authors came
like George Saunders, Anthony Joyer,

00:27:16.654 --> 00:27:21.181
I had the chance to listen to these people
who never came to Canada, in particular,

00:27:21.181 --> 00:27:23.146
and got to meet some of them as well.

00:27:23.146 --> 00:27:28.686
And some of those conversations I had and
advice I got, it made me more confident

00:27:28.686 --> 00:27:34.570
to ultimately invest in myself and
invest in this dream that I always had.

00:27:34.570 --> 00:27:39.174
And then one other thing that I'd add in
terms of extracurriculars, it was related

00:27:39.174 --> 00:27:43.580
to a course I took in anthropology and
psychiatry as part of Stanford.

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:48.407
But I ended up spending eight
weeks working with formerly

00:27:48.407 --> 00:27:53.360
homeless adults in San Mateo,
on improv therapy.

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:56.033
And so every week we'd carpool over and

00:27:56.033 --> 00:28:01.703
work with these adults who were in various
shapes in terms of their mental health and

00:28:01.703 --> 00:28:05.450
it was a really,
really impactful experience for me.

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:10.726
And I was able to apply a lot of what
I learned, not just in that class but

00:28:10.726 --> 00:28:14.663
from GSB,
to trying to connect with these people and

00:28:14.663 --> 00:28:17.275
something I always bring with me.

00:28:17.275 --> 00:28:20.964
&gt;&gt; Thank you, this next question,
Sam, I'll have you kick it off and

00:28:20.964 --> 00:28:23.470
then maybe we'll go over to Jackie next.

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:28.490
But starting with you, Sam, how did you
find career support at the Stanford GSB?

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:31.769
Did you leverage the Career Management
Center or any of our other centers,

00:28:31.769 --> 00:28:35.361
like our Center for Entrepreneurial
Studies, or Center for Social Innovation,

00:28:35.361 --> 00:28:36.700
or other parts of the campus?

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:39.495
How did you kind of go about
that career support process?

00:28:39.495 --> 00:28:41.390
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll give a big pitch for this.

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:43.858
One thing I didn't expect at the GSB is,
Lisa,

00:28:43.858 --> 00:28:49.060
you can correct me if I'm wrong here, but
the GSB's staff to student ratio is crazy.

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:53.677
It felt one to one, it was probably
three or four to one, but there were so

00:28:53.677 --> 00:28:58.020
many fantastic people whose job
is to provide student support.

00:28:58.020 --> 00:29:01.858
And so those three centers Lisa listed,
I think I used all of them.

00:29:01.858 --> 00:29:06.646
Entrepreneurial Studies coordinates
a bunch of really great classes and

00:29:06.646 --> 00:29:10.968
does a bunch of research on starting
startups, that I as a lawyer,

00:29:10.968 --> 00:29:14.150
read in great detail
before starting a company.

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:20.834
The CMC is amazing, I remember Law School
there was the law firm people who

00:29:20.834 --> 00:29:26.153
told you how to get a law firm job, and
there was the public interest people,

00:29:26.153 --> 00:29:31.220
who told you how to get one of three
types of public interest jobs.

00:29:31.220 --> 00:29:36.762
GSB had 17 different specialties
you could select from.

00:29:36.762 --> 00:29:40.457
Grace Yokoi and their team, I remember
I spent way too much time with her just

00:29:40.457 --> 00:29:43.074
being, how do I start a startup,
this seems insane.

00:29:43.074 --> 00:29:46.670
And she really helped give me
the confidence to do that.

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:50.792
And the general vibe was just such
a choose your own adventure and

00:29:50.792 --> 00:29:52.100
they were so great.

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:55.836
The staff have such a reservoir of
knowledge about who good alumni are to

00:29:55.836 --> 00:29:59.399
talk to, what good classes are to take,
it really meant a lot to me.

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:03.050
&gt;&gt; Jackie, how about over to you?

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:06.891
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's amazing, I also used
all of the centers you mentioned.

00:30:06.891 --> 00:30:11.367
The Center for Social Innovation was
fantastic, they had a fellowship between

00:30:11.367 --> 00:30:14.460
your first and
second years that a lot of students did.

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:20.590
Or a number of students that were
creating social entrepreneurial ventures.

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:24.826
And then they coupled that with
an internship where you could work for

00:30:24.826 --> 00:30:28.347
an impact organization,
I worked in impact investing.

00:30:28.347 --> 00:30:31.730
And there was tons of funding for
both of those.

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:34.466
I still talk to the staff pretty
regularly from Center for

00:30:34.466 --> 00:30:38.090
Social Innovation on mentorship and
they're so helpful.

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:43.385
I also found the peer support incredibly
helpful for career, especially because,

00:30:43.385 --> 00:30:48.620
like Lisa mentioned, I think about a fifth
of classmates start their own venture.

00:30:48.620 --> 00:30:52.294
And so, while you could absolutely
go into consulting or finance,

00:30:52.294 --> 00:30:56.549
I didn't feel any pressure to not do
something entrepreneurial because there

00:30:56.549 --> 00:30:59.274
were tons of other students
starting new things.

00:30:59.274 --> 00:31:03.307
And so it didn't feel as crazy or scary
since everyone else was bootstrapping or

00:31:03.307 --> 00:31:04.650
fundraising.

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:10.937
And then a couple more,
the Career Center was really helpful.

00:31:10.937 --> 00:31:13.930
Especially in my first year I was
interested in venture capital and

00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:16.067
investing and
I had no investing experience and

00:31:16.067 --> 00:31:17.626
income from the nonprofit world.

00:31:17.626 --> 00:31:22.583
And the staff was so helpful at
understanding the world of investing and

00:31:22.583 --> 00:31:27.630
how to couch your experience in
ways we've got opportunities.

00:31:27.630 --> 00:31:29.016
A professor Rob Siegel,

00:31:29.016 --> 00:31:33.050
let me work with his venture capital
fund to get exposure during school.

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:39.008
There was also an accelerator called
Start-X where you could get financial aid

00:31:39.008 --> 00:31:41.170
and accelerator wraparound support.

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:45.222
And then the last thing I'll mention is
that for people that go into the nonprofit

00:31:45.222 --> 00:31:49.584
sector or the public sector, there's loan
forgiveness, which was really helpful.

00:31:49.584 --> 00:31:53.641
So I receive, probably $9,000
a year now to repay my loans,

00:31:53.641 --> 00:31:58.348
which is super helpful and it's not
taxed and that's also really helpful for

00:31:58.348 --> 00:32:01.340
pursuing a career in
social entrepreneurship.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:03.960
Great, thank you.

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:06.970
This next question, Jack,
I'll maybe have you kick us off.

00:32:06.970 --> 00:32:08.630
And then over to Rebecca next and

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:12.020
anyone else who wants to chime in
because I know this was a popular one.

00:32:12.020 --> 00:32:15.391
But how have you leveraged
your classmates, alumni,

00:32:15.391 --> 00:32:18.321
faculty or
the Stanford network more broadly,

00:32:18.321 --> 00:32:23.240
either professionally speaking, right,
or even just personally speaking?

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:24.533
Jack, you want to start us off?

00:32:24.533 --> 00:32:28.958
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, I think there's
no way ,like Sam brought up

00:32:28.958 --> 00:32:32.777
how powerful the GSB was
in starting his business.

00:32:32.777 --> 00:32:37.804
Totally feel the same way
from meeting people in my

00:32:37.804 --> 00:32:42.709
industry, to advice on
how to set up my company,

00:32:42.709 --> 00:32:47.514
to I've hired a couple
GSBers years behind me.

00:32:47.514 --> 00:32:52.200
I think maybe one very tangible
thing is on fundraising.

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:56.178
So, I'd say somewhere between 20 and

00:32:56.178 --> 00:33:01.443
50 million of fundraising
has been from Stanford,

00:33:01.443 --> 00:33:08.360
either classmates, referrals from
classmates, and other alumni.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:15.292
So, it's been massively impactful
to me across the board from advice,

00:33:15.292 --> 00:33:20.190
to hiring, to fundraising,
to all sorts of ways.

00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:25.371
So, there's just no way my business and
my career would be

00:33:25.371 --> 00:33:31.422
where it was if I hadn't started
the business while I was at the GSB.

00:33:31.422 --> 00:33:32.029
&gt;&gt; Rebecca.

00:33:32.029 --> 00:33:33.818
&gt;&gt; I echo all of those things.

00:33:33.818 --> 00:33:37.808
And I think, I mean,
I would say in general,

00:33:37.808 --> 00:33:44.003
one of the things that maybe attracts
people to Stanford and the GSB but

00:33:44.003 --> 00:33:51.143
then is really the seeds are watered when
you're there, is a level of comfort and

00:33:51.143 --> 00:33:56.605
openness to reaching out to
your classmates with questions,

00:33:56.605 --> 00:34:03.250
for help with the moments when you
need it, to celebrate their successes.

00:34:03.250 --> 00:34:08.876
That at a level that I don't
see in many other communities,

00:34:08.876 --> 00:34:11.360
especially of that size.

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:16.442
So I think that there's just
a base level of care and

00:34:16.442 --> 00:34:20.459
a sense of community that's created and

00:34:20.459 --> 00:34:26.970
further cultivated within the class
that really stays with you.

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:32.456
So that it's like, I would say to
any specific situation personal,

00:34:32.456 --> 00:34:37.657
professional, that my top five
phone numbers that I would call,

00:34:37.657 --> 00:34:43.636
different situation is probably going to
be somebody who I know from the GSB.

00:34:43.636 --> 00:34:50.011
And I mean, I have a few great friends
who we're now parents at the same school,

00:34:50.011 --> 00:34:56.120
we go for weekly runs together with some
of them, I met my husband at the GSB.

00:34:56.120 --> 00:35:02.337
So that's pretty transformational and
I think then specifically,

00:35:02.337 --> 00:35:06.953
particularly for
me at career transition points.

00:35:06.953 --> 00:35:13.051
That's when I kind of reached out beyond
the closest concentric circle and

00:35:13.051 --> 00:35:16.380
tapped into kind of a broader network.

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:24.268
I have a GSB alum from many years from
before my class is on my board right now.

00:35:24.268 --> 00:35:29.925
So those kind of moments I
think there's such a deep,

00:35:29.925 --> 00:35:36.728
great network and people have
always been really happy to help.

00:35:36.728 --> 00:35:41.034
&gt;&gt; I can also chime in if there's time,
and I love everything you shared, Rebecca.

00:35:41.034 --> 00:35:46.118
I was going to add there's a program that
student organized at the GSB called Talk,

00:35:46.118 --> 00:35:50.929
where students give about a 40 minute
talk about anything in their lives.

00:35:50.929 --> 00:35:55.276
And I found that incredibly helpful
because people know what you care about

00:35:55.276 --> 00:35:57.693
and can support you the rest of your time.

00:35:57.693 --> 00:36:02.270
When I was at the GSB, I knew I wanted
to work in women's sexual wellness and

00:36:02.270 --> 00:36:07.288
sex tech, which was absolutely terrifying
for me because it was very stigmatized

00:36:07.288 --> 00:36:11.965
area and I didn't know anyone else with
an MBA that was going into that path.

00:36:11.965 --> 00:36:16.862
Although now, this year, six Harvard and
Stanford MBAs have reached out to

00:36:16.862 --> 00:36:21.777
me that are working in women sex tech,
which is so exciting two years later.

00:36:21.777 --> 00:36:26.710
But I felt that it was a great practice
ground to be talking about topics that

00:36:26.710 --> 00:36:30.702
terrified me, both in class and
in Talk, and socially, and

00:36:30.702 --> 00:36:34.409
really prepared me to get
the confidence to then write.

00:36:34.409 --> 00:36:37.069
And there are times out
about those subjects and

00:36:37.069 --> 00:36:39.405
now I have no problem talking about them.

00:36:39.405 --> 00:36:44.430
Whether it's in a sex discrimination
lawsuit, or a meeting with pretty much,

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:47.655
anyone but
without those two years in a safe space,

00:36:47.655 --> 00:36:50.214
I don't know how I would have done that.

00:36:50.214 --> 00:36:52.048
And then as an alum, you have so

00:36:52.048 --> 00:36:56.860
many WhatsApp groups where we have a
women's founder group that meets monthly.

00:36:56.860 --> 00:36:59.952
We have entrepreneur groups,
we have Bay Area groups.

00:36:59.952 --> 00:37:02.590
And so
you can always send a question like,

00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:07.354
I need a documentary lawyer or I'm
researching competitors in this market,

00:37:07.354 --> 00:37:10.970
and everyone will know all
the CEOs in some startup space.

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:16.044
We just had a classmate of mine that
a year out of school successfully

00:37:16.044 --> 00:37:21.742
led a shareholder activist approach to
get three sustainability experts on

00:37:21.742 --> 00:37:27.541
the board of Exxon Mobil to move Exxon
Mobil to more sustainable directions.

00:37:27.541 --> 00:37:31.241
And we had a whole webinar where he talked
to all of our classmates off the record

00:37:31.241 --> 00:37:33.102
about their strategies and learning.

00:37:33.102 --> 00:37:37.793
So, we're still very much in contact
personally and professionally.

00:37:37.793 --> 00:37:42.474
And I also found, as a woman at the GSB
pursuing those areas I shared about,

00:37:42.474 --> 00:37:47.232
men were so helpful, classmates connected
me with book publishers because

00:37:47.232 --> 00:37:51.275
they thought that you should write
a book about these subjects.

00:37:51.275 --> 00:37:56.110
And really, men as well as women were
just incredibly personally supportive in

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:57.781
a way that meant a lot to me.

00:38:00.061 --> 00:38:03.976
&gt;&gt; Let's go to some of the questions from
our audience here because we've had a few

00:38:03.976 --> 00:38:04.860
submitted now.

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:07.320
So, here's a question from Mike Close.

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:10.415
He says, wow, incredible to hear
all these different experiences.

00:38:10.415 --> 00:38:14.756
How do you kind of resist getting caught
up in the more traditional recruiting

00:38:14.756 --> 00:38:19.431
tracks in business school, since it can be
difficult to balance your passions with

00:38:19.431 --> 00:38:21.525
kind of practicality and finances?

00:38:21.525 --> 00:38:23.962
So, anyone want to tackle that one?

00:38:29.859 --> 00:38:31.525
&gt;&gt; I could take one stab.

00:38:31.525 --> 00:38:36.280
I mean, I think my wife and
I were both going to school, and so

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:42.343
we were both paying a lot of tuition and
I definitely felt that pressure to go,

00:38:42.343 --> 00:38:48.160
in my case, what I was concerned is
going back to the firm where I was at,.

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:54.590
And we ended up taking out some loans and
stuff to figure out how to do it.

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:58.042
But I will say Stanford was super helpful.

00:38:58.042 --> 00:39:02.921
There's lots of programs that folks
have mentioned about if you're

00:39:02.921 --> 00:39:08.052
going into nonprofit, there's extra
incentive sort of to do that, and

00:39:08.052 --> 00:39:13.876
lots of fellowships and other things that
I know my classmates took advantage of.

00:39:13.876 --> 00:39:18.811
And I just felt financially there
was a lot of resources there to help

00:39:18.811 --> 00:39:20.630
figure that out.

00:39:20.630 --> 00:39:25.807
And then the culture of my class was so
supportive that just in

00:39:25.807 --> 00:39:30.883
terms of the FOMO of what you're
going to do with your career,

00:39:30.883 --> 00:39:37.097
I just can't imagine a better place
to do something off the beaten path.

00:39:37.097 --> 00:39:41.417
Because so many people were
sort of joining you in that and

00:39:41.417 --> 00:39:46.367
that alone helped everyone do what
they were passionate about and

00:39:46.367 --> 00:39:51.870
not feel sort of pressured to do what
everyone else was doing basically.

00:39:51.870 --> 00:39:53.612
I don't know.

00:39:53.612 --> 00:39:56.525
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'd echo what Jack said and
just add briefly,

00:39:56.525 --> 00:40:01.600
there were a bunch of classes and all my
favorite ones at GSB fit this description.

00:40:01.600 --> 00:40:05.278
That would have one alum per class period
come in and sort of be a guest speaker,

00:40:05.278 --> 00:40:08.740
either to make a case about them,
sometimes they just talk.

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:13.462
And that was really what gave me
confidence because it feels a lot less

00:40:13.462 --> 00:40:18.534
crazy once you've met 12 people
who are yeah, I was in your shoes,

00:40:18.534 --> 00:40:24.245
two, five, ten years ago and this was
scary and now I did it this is where I am.

00:40:24.245 --> 00:40:29.212
I would never have gone off
the beaten track without that.

00:40:29.212 --> 00:40:32.437
&gt;&gt; I also had great advice from
a classmate, the first year of GSB.

00:40:32.437 --> 00:40:36.637
I was deciding whether to do
interviews for consulting or not but

00:40:36.637 --> 00:40:41.611
I knew that I had these other passions and
she had the idea give it six months or

00:40:41.611 --> 00:40:45.500
eight months where you just let
yourself fully focus on your

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:49.039
entrepreneurial area and
then assess in six months.

00:40:49.039 --> 00:40:52.743
So, you can always apply for
consulting in your second year of MBA but,

00:40:52.743 --> 00:40:56.220
set goals for yourself and
then see where you're at at that point.

00:40:56.220 --> 00:41:00.537
And six months in, I was so in love with
my work and the goals had all shifted and

00:41:00.537 --> 00:41:04.720
they'd all changed but
you knew there was no going back.

00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:07.139
So that peer support
was incredibly helpful.

00:41:07.139 --> 00:41:11.088
And on the finances, like I mentioned,
the loan forgiveness did help.

00:41:11.088 --> 00:41:13.350
There were different fellowships
that you could apply for.

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:18.249
They were obviously competitive, but
a couple of my course mate got I think

00:41:18.249 --> 00:41:23.326
$110,000 to create a nonprofit and
there was a few others like that.

00:41:23.326 --> 00:41:26.378
Plus the network that you're
getting is incredibly helpful,

00:41:26.378 --> 00:41:28.923
like others have mentioned for
their fundraising.

00:41:28.923 --> 00:41:35.224
So, there's ways to bolster that
financial opportunities and

00:41:35.224 --> 00:41:41.999
I think that it just definitely was
a helpful financial investment.

00:41:41.999 --> 00:41:44.245
&gt;&gt; Here's another question,
it says looking back,

00:41:44.245 --> 00:41:47.389
what's one thing you would do
differently during your time at the GSB?

00:41:54.638 --> 00:41:56.517
Who wants to take that one?

00:41:59.859 --> 00:42:03.632
I can try.
I think looking back if it was something

00:42:03.632 --> 00:42:07.290
that was really pounded
into us at the GSB.

00:42:07.290 --> 00:42:09.999
So it wasn't as if
the opportunities weren't there but

00:42:09.999 --> 00:42:11.563
honestly just take more risks.

00:42:11.563 --> 00:42:16.453
It is such a risk free
environment in that way.

00:42:16.453 --> 00:42:21.820
And it is the opportunity to really
have all of these incredible

00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:27.300
people around you, professors,
and alums, and classmates.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:30.070
And you really have the opportunity to ask

00:42:30.070 --> 00:42:34.993
all the dumb questions that you want to
ask and just pick people's brains.

00:42:34.993 --> 00:42:39.815
And I think having either saying
that you're a student at the GSB is

00:42:39.815 --> 00:42:43.700
a huge door opener for
talking to all sorts of people.

00:42:43.700 --> 00:42:47.617
And at the time,
I think it seems a little risky, but

00:42:47.617 --> 00:42:53.866
really compared to what things are like in
the real world outside of the GSB bubble.

00:42:53.866 --> 00:42:58.797
It's really a great opportunity to take
advantage of having those two years to

00:42:58.797 --> 00:43:03.504
just experiment and play around and
just talk to a bunch of different people

00:43:03.504 --> 00:43:06.657
to sort of figure out where
it is that you want to go.

00:43:06.657 --> 00:43:09.921
Not just in the one or
two years after school but

00:43:09.921 --> 00:43:13.603
in the next 5, 10,
15 years because I think and

00:43:13.603 --> 00:43:19.146
I know some of the questions that have
come through have asked about pivoting.

00:43:19.146 --> 00:43:22.643
And I think, all of us probably
had very clear goals and

00:43:22.643 --> 00:43:27.369
ideas of what we were going to do with
the rest of our lives when we applied.

00:43:27.369 --> 00:43:31.297
And then a lot of times that changed,
it changed for me while I was at the GSB.

00:43:31.297 --> 00:43:33.915
And then it changed again
five years after, and

00:43:33.915 --> 00:43:36.151
it changed again five years after that.

00:43:36.151 --> 00:43:40.337
And so, it's good to kind of
have all of those skills and

00:43:40.337 --> 00:43:43.734
experience from the GSB
that you can draw on.

00:43:43.734 --> 00:43:48.018
&gt;&gt; Anything else that you do differently,
anyone at the GSB,

00:43:48.018 --> 00:43:49.997
knowing what you know now?

00:43:49.997 --> 00:43:51.796
&gt;&gt; I didn't do a global study trip.

00:43:51.796 --> 00:43:52.785
I regret that.

00:43:52.785 --> 00:43:55.820
[LAUGH] That's already been mentioned.

00:43:59.680 --> 00:44:00.723
&gt;&gt; All right, well,

00:44:00.723 --> 00:44:05.155
maybe we'll pivot off of what Emily just
was talking about with career pivots.

00:44:05.155 --> 00:44:09.763
So we do have some questions here about
all of you mentioned that in one way or

00:44:09.763 --> 00:44:14.521
another Stanford GSB has offered an
opportunity to pivot from your old career

00:44:14.521 --> 00:44:16.240
path into a new one.

00:44:16.240 --> 00:44:20.231
Looking back now, did you know exactly
what you wanted to end up in post MBA kind

00:44:20.231 --> 00:44:21.743
of in your current role now and

00:44:21.743 --> 00:44:24.421
how you would leverage your
GSB degree to get there?

00:44:24.421 --> 00:44:28.098
Or was this sort of an unplanned
serendipitous process that

00:44:28.098 --> 00:44:29.648
led you to where you are?

00:44:29.648 --> 00:44:32.997
So, does anyone want to talk
about their path or the pivots?

00:44:46.918 --> 00:44:50.934
&gt;&gt; I can find,
[LAUGH] you want to go ahead Jackie?

00:44:50.934 --> 00:44:53.635
&gt;&gt; No, I can go after you.

00:44:53.635 --> 00:44:54.239
&gt;&gt; Okay, so

00:44:54.239 --> 00:44:59.280
I wouldn't say necessarily when I went
to GSB I made this pivot that I wanted.

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:00.709
I wanted to become a writer, right.

00:45:00.709 --> 00:45:02.958
And to be clear after this last year,

00:45:02.958 --> 00:45:06.015
I definitely don't want
to be a full time writer.

00:45:06.015 --> 00:45:08.965
And what I will say is that
during GSB it made me more

00:45:08.965 --> 00:45:11.364
confident to take risks with my career.

00:45:11.364 --> 00:45:14.949
And lot of that has to do with
what Jack and Sam said earlier,

00:45:14.949 --> 00:45:19.256
the culture where you saw people
taking these risks, where people felt

00:45:19.256 --> 00:45:24.170
comfortable talking about failures, and
failing in startups and coming back.

00:45:24.170 --> 00:45:28.900
And so, that's something where, that
made me decide to leave publishing for

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:33.559
a period of time feeling comfortable
that I knew that world well enough that,

00:45:33.559 --> 00:45:38.433
if I had something that was worthy of
being published even if maybe there's a 1%

00:45:38.433 --> 00:45:40.538
chance of that happening or less.

00:45:40.538 --> 00:45:45.053
That I was comfortable taking those
odds on with what I believed I had and

00:45:45.053 --> 00:45:49.126
being comfortable that I can
recover if things don't work out.

00:45:49.126 --> 00:45:52.622
And so, I think that makes me
more comfortable even now as I'm

00:45:52.622 --> 00:45:54.112
transitioning, right?

00:45:54.112 --> 00:45:57.397
You could argue, do I even know what
I want to do 20 years from now,

00:45:57.397 --> 00:46:01.045
I would honestly say that I wouldn't
be able to predict that at the moment.

00:46:01.045 --> 00:46:05.370
But I am comfortable that no matter
what happens based on my track record,

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:09.511
based on my GSB experience,
that I'll be able to bounce back from it.

00:46:09.511 --> 00:46:13.435
And I think that's one of the biggest
thing that GSB taught me about pivots is,

00:46:13.435 --> 00:46:16.243
yes, there are all the tactical
things that you can do.

00:46:16.243 --> 00:46:21.248
There are the steps that you can take like
I won a short story competition run by

00:46:21.248 --> 00:46:26.253
The Guardian and by Harper Collins before
I decided to take time off, right,

00:46:26.253 --> 00:46:30.878
I actually got into my top tier MFA
program and I'm about halfway done.

00:46:30.878 --> 00:46:33.231
It's a remote one so
I can do it during work.

00:46:33.231 --> 00:46:35.488
But I build support networks around me.

00:46:35.488 --> 00:46:39.638
And so, there are tactical ways in which
GSB helped me think about these things.

00:46:39.638 --> 00:46:41.689
So I do things systematically.

00:46:41.689 --> 00:46:45.850
But I think the biggest thing is
the courage to be able to do pivots

00:46:45.850 --> 00:46:48.624
because you see other people doing it, and

00:46:48.624 --> 00:46:53.268
it becomes a little less daunting and
helps you build that confidence.

00:46:53.268 --> 00:46:58.175
&gt;&gt; Yeah that's so beautiful,
and for me, there was a lot of

00:46:58.175 --> 00:47:03.503
serendipity that led to finding
new passions in grad school.

00:47:03.503 --> 00:47:06.393
When I applied I probably
thought I wanted to work for

00:47:06.393 --> 00:47:08.969
a foundation in international development.

00:47:08.969 --> 00:47:13.732
And now what I'm doing is completely
different because I had several personal

00:47:13.732 --> 00:47:18.424
experience in grad school that made me
really passionate about ending sexual

00:47:18.424 --> 00:47:21.107
assault and creating a more equal culture.

00:47:21.107 --> 00:47:25.295
I started business school and a month
into it the Me Too Movement kicked off.

00:47:25.295 --> 00:47:29.423
So all of those things
impacted my career very much.

00:47:29.423 --> 00:47:33.022
And then, we were thinking
about a question earlier about,

00:47:33.022 --> 00:47:36.552
how to prepare for
a career in a non-traditional path, but

00:47:36.552 --> 00:47:39.602
I think it's really about
finding your passions.

00:47:39.602 --> 00:47:43.372
And then when people see you doing
what you're passionate about,

00:47:43.372 --> 00:47:47.085
others notice your excitement and
possibility and get on board.

00:47:47.085 --> 00:47:51.055
I also feel that right now, I am two
years out of GSB, but I'm preparing for

00:47:51.055 --> 00:47:54.458
another career pivot where I've
been a social entrepreneur and

00:47:54.458 --> 00:47:56.238
founded a couple of nonprofits.

00:47:56.238 --> 00:48:00.400
But I realized recently that I'm
really passionate about writing and

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:03.089
that there's a few books
that I want to write.

00:48:03.089 --> 00:48:04.550
Some are about.

00:48:04.550 --> 00:48:07.376
Law, and I don't have a law degree.

00:48:07.376 --> 00:48:11.757
And my professor that I mentioned
earlier from the writing class is really

00:48:11.757 --> 00:48:15.160
supportive of me going into
investigative journalism.

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:17.137
And I didn't know any of that.

00:48:17.137 --> 00:48:19.790
I didn't really know any
professional writers, so

00:48:19.790 --> 00:48:22.092
it's cool hearing your story too, Yiming.

00:48:22.092 --> 00:48:25.916
But I've been just putting in place
the foundations to apply to different

00:48:25.916 --> 00:48:30.001
fellowships or to try to structure my
finances to figure out how to do writing.

00:48:30.001 --> 00:48:34.321
And while I didn't do a ton of writing
other than that class during the GSB,

00:48:34.321 --> 00:48:38.587
I definitely echo that you develop
this sense that anything is possible.

00:48:38.587 --> 00:48:41.797
You develop the sense that
you can learn new skills.

00:48:41.797 --> 00:48:46.708
And that you can dive into a new area.

00:48:46.708 --> 00:48:51.452
Because every day at the GSB, it feels
like you can just create whatever you

00:48:51.452 --> 00:48:56.060
want to create, because you're seeing
people that are doing that every day.

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:02.254
&gt;&gt; Maybe pivoting off of something that
Jackie said, and I think Yiming you also

00:49:02.254 --> 00:49:05.102
talked about it maybe having
some interest in the ed school.

00:49:05.102 --> 00:49:08.870
And let's see, we've got Sam who did
the law degree, the joint degree.

00:49:08.870 --> 00:49:12.530
How did any of you leverage other
parts of Stanford University,

00:49:12.530 --> 00:49:17.090
either taking classes there or connecting
with students or programs or talks?

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:20.717
Talk a little bit more broadly about
your Stanford wide experience.

00:49:24.421 --> 00:49:26.730
&gt;&gt; Yeah, just briefly share sort
of the joint degree perspective.

00:49:26.730 --> 00:49:29.422
I think there were,
felt like, maybe a quarter or

00:49:29.422 --> 00:49:33.282
a fifth of my class were joint degrees and
with a ton of different stuff.

00:49:33.282 --> 00:49:36.508
So it's pretty easy once you
get to Stanford to apply to do

00:49:36.508 --> 00:49:39.075
a joint degree than
almost any other school.

00:49:39.075 --> 00:49:41.404
They all combine neatly with
the business schools, so

00:49:41.404 --> 00:49:42.930
you got to take some cross classes.

00:49:42.930 --> 00:49:44.345
There's a community of
people who are doing that.

00:49:44.345 --> 00:49:49.055
So there was the MD MBAs,
many of them are doctors now.

00:49:49.055 --> 00:49:54.920
The JD MBAs, there were PhD MBAs,
which seems insane to me.

00:49:54.920 --> 00:49:59.654
But there was a huge mix, and the school
Stanford, I think, much more than most

00:49:59.654 --> 00:50:04.248
universities, really is integrated there
in a way that the departments work

00:50:04.248 --> 00:50:08.400
together to coordinate stuff and
made the scheduling easy.

00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:11.572
And so for
anyone who's considering that just know it

00:50:11.572 --> 00:50:16.097
was made really easy to do it and a lot
of folks get to school and then add that.

00:50:19.755 --> 00:50:23.802
&gt;&gt; Maybe one last super quick
question in our last minute or so.

00:50:23.802 --> 00:50:27.503
What is an experience that you
could have only had at Stanford?

00:50:36.264 --> 00:50:40.894
&gt;&gt; I definitely think talk the experience
of hearing hundreds of classmates

00:50:40.894 --> 00:50:45.821
share about most vulnerable experiences
of their lives and be so supportive,

00:50:45.821 --> 00:50:47.781
for me was transformational.

00:50:47.781 --> 00:50:52.457
There's such a culture of vulnerability
at the GSB and of true support.

00:50:52.457 --> 00:50:53.758
And I just thought, okay,

00:50:53.758 --> 00:50:56.883
my classmates will support all
of my other classmates' topics.

00:50:56.883 --> 00:51:00.542
But I really honestly went into my talk
thinking everyone's going to hate me

00:51:00.542 --> 00:51:03.611
after I give a talk about female
sexuality for 40 minutes and

00:51:03.611 --> 00:51:05.640
actually it was the opposite.

00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:10.340
Everyone was so supportive and I felt like
I belonged at the GSB because it really

00:51:10.340 --> 00:51:12.597
created a feeling of being accepted.

00:51:12.597 --> 00:51:15.873
&gt;&gt; Do want to recognize
that we are getting to,

00:51:15.873 --> 00:51:18.972
pardon me, the end of our session here.

00:51:18.972 --> 00:51:21.441
And I want to thank all
of our panelists for

00:51:21.441 --> 00:51:25.010
sharing your perspectives
today in your various fields.

00:51:25.010 --> 00:51:28.724
I think there's just tremendous diversity
represented in your backgrounds and

00:51:28.724 --> 00:51:29.660
your experiences.

00:51:29.660 --> 00:51:34.428
And again, it just highlights how
wonderfully flexible this MBA degree in

00:51:34.428 --> 00:51:37.150
educational experience is.

00:51:37.150 --> 00:51:41.210
I also want to thank all of our attendees
today for your interest in Stanford.

00:51:41.210 --> 00:51:44.633
We hope that this is just the start
of your Stanford GSB journey and

00:51:44.633 --> 00:51:47.350
we definitely invite you to continue it.

00:51:47.350 --> 00:51:51.840
One great thing you can do is go to
mystanford.MBA if you haven't been there

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:52.890
already.

00:51:52.890 --> 00:51:56.564
You can create a personalized dashboard
by taking a really quick quiz.

00:51:56.564 --> 00:52:00.501
And that will help you learn
a little bit more about students or

00:52:00.501 --> 00:52:05.860
alumni who might have similar interests
as you do, events that are coming up.

00:52:05.860 --> 00:52:07.098
If you start an application,

00:52:07.098 --> 00:52:10.070
it'll I'll track your application
progress along the way.

00:52:10.070 --> 00:52:14.674
So just lots of wonderful opportunities
to continue to learn more about

00:52:14.674 --> 00:52:16.780
Stanford at mystanford.mba.

00:52:16.780 --> 00:52:20.053
Again, thank you to everyone for
attending today and

00:52:20.053 --> 00:52:24.571
best of luck in your journey for
an off the beaten path career, thank you.

00:52:24.571 --> 00:52:29.392
[MUSIC]

